WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.590
Picture a family packing their home for a move.

2
00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:11.365
Every box gets a card taped to it that lists what's inside, so nothing goes missing on the way.

3
00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:16.710
MoveMate is a small program that helps them, and prints a card for each kind of thing.

4
00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:21.765
For a lamp: its name, desk lamp, one bulb, and its shade, null, because it has none.

5
00:00:21.933 --> 00:00:23.594
We wrote each card by hand.

6
00:00:23.594 --> 00:00:28.453
The class lists the properties, and a small function repeats that list as text.

7
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.082
Then the lamps gained a property, watts.

8
00:00:31.082 --> 00:00:32.571
Nobody touched the card.

9
00:00:32.571 --> 00:00:34.743
What happens when we build and run?

10
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.437
The build is green, and the program runs.

11
00:00:40.437 --> 00:00:44.745
The lamp knows it draws 40 watts, and its card keeps that a secret.

12
00:00:44.745 --> 00:00:46.417
Nothing printed a warning.

13
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.748
Every word on the card is still true.

14
00:00:48.748 --> 00:00:53.103
It's just not the whole truth, like a moving quote that forgets the stairs.

15
00:00:53.103 --> 00:00:56.238
The compiler checks that each name on the card exists.

16
00:00:56.238 --> 00:00:58.851
It can't check that the card says everything.

17
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.591
That gap has a name: the card has drifted from its class.

18
00:01:02.591 --> 00:01:06.245
And every class you add is one more card waiting to drift.

19
00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:10.049
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

20
00:01:10.049 --> 00:01:12.140
People say a tool generates code.

21
00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:13.281
Generates it when?

22
00:01:13.281 --> 00:01:16.576
Where does it go, if it's never in my source folder?

23
00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:18.921
And how would that keep a card right?

24
00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:21.516
First, three moments.

25
00:01:21.516 --> 00:01:26.418
The build is everything Gradle does to turn your source into a program.

26
00:01:26.418 --> 00:01:30.491
Compile time is one step of it: source becomes class files.

27
00:01:30.491 --> 00:01:33.597
Run time comes later, when the JVM runs them.

28
00:01:33.733 --> 00:01:38.524
The hand-written card came before the build, from a person, and people forget.

29
00:01:38.524 --> 00:01:43.560
Reflection works it out at run time: the program reads its own fields, every time.

30
00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:44.994
It never forgets.

31
00:01:44.994 --> 00:01:52.039
But it pays on every call: about 220 nanoseconds a card, against 25 by hand, on a busy machine.

32
00:01:52.039 --> 00:01:57.897
Like unpacking every box at the new house, each time someone asks for the list.

33
00:01:58.067 --> 00:02:02.131
Wouldn't it be nice to have the speed of one and the memory of the other?

34
00:02:02.131 --> 00:02:06.251
That's the third spot: a program writes the cards, once, during the build.

35
00:02:07.133 --> 00:02:08.280
Back to the house.

36
00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.508
Your source files are sealed boxes.

37
00:02:10.508 --> 00:02:16.112
The label on top lists the class name and each property with its type: the declarations.

38
00:02:16.112 --> 00:02:18.851
The code inside the functions stays sealed.

39
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.900
Stick a note on a box: make an inventory card for this one.

40
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:27.791
In code, that's an annotation, @Inventory, a note written with an at sign.

41
00:02:27.791 --> 00:02:30.369
It does nothing until someone reads it.

42
00:02:30.533 --> 00:02:37.436
A helper with a clipboard walks the house, finds each box with that note, reads its label, and writes a new card.

43
00:02:37.436 --> 00:02:45.011
That helper is an annotation processor: a program that runs during the build and writes new source files from what it reads.

44
00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.043
When the van leaves, the boxes and the new cards go in together.

45
00:02:49.043 --> 00:02:54.387
That's code generation: a program writes source code that's compiled together with yours.

46
00:02:54.533 --> 00:02:56.633
And the helper never gets bored.

47
00:02:56.633 --> 00:03:02.867
Card forty gets the same care as card one, because it reads every label afresh, on every build.

48
00:03:03.067 --> 00:03:04.021
Now the name.

49
00:03:04.021 --> 00:03:10.701
KSP, Kotlin Symbol Processing, is a tool from Google that runs such helpers on Kotlin code.

50
00:03:10.701 --> 00:03:13.711
A symbol is any named thing in your code.

51
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:17.824
Let's watch a ready-made helper in a real build.

52
00:03:17.824 --> 00:03:23.668
The lab's playground holds the four MoveMate classes, each with the note, and no cards.

53
00:03:23.668 --> 00:03:25.415
We run the KSP task alone.

54
00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.616
How many files will it write?

55
00:03:27.616 --> 00:03:28.728
Pause and guess.

56
00:03:31.733 --> 00:03:34.005
Four, one per class with the note.

57
00:03:34.005 --> 00:03:36.810
And they land under build, generated, KSP.

58
00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:40.351
Never in your source folder: that one belongs to you.

59
00:03:40.533 --> 00:03:46.449
Open the lamp's card: our hand-written card, line for line, with one comment on top.

60
00:03:46.449 --> 00:03:47.858
Generated from Lamp.

61
00:03:47.858 --> 00:03:48.703
Do not edit.

62
00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:50.214
And when?

63
00:03:50.214 --> 00:03:52.806
Gradle lists a build's steps in order.

64
00:03:52.806 --> 00:03:57.514
First it builds the helper: a processor must exist before it can run.

65
00:03:57.514 --> 00:03:58.742
Then the KSP task.

66
00:03:58.742 --> 00:04:03.927
Only then the Kotlin compiler, with your classes and the new cards together.

67
00:04:04.067 --> 00:04:05.506
Now the real test.

68
00:04:05.506 --> 00:04:09.186
Add watts to Lamp, and run the KSP task again.

69
00:04:09.186 --> 00:04:11.105
Nobody touches any card.

70
00:04:11.267 --> 00:04:13.789
The new card gains watts, all by itself.

71
00:04:13.789 --> 00:04:16.185
Call it, and it says: watts equals 40.

72
00:04:16.185 --> 00:04:19.905
The hand-written card from before still stops at the shade.

73
00:04:21.267 --> 00:04:22.968
Here the picture breaks.

74
00:04:22.968 --> 00:04:26.369
A real helper could lift a flap and peek inside.

75
00:04:26.369 --> 00:04:30.834
KSP can't: it reads declarations, never the code in a function.

76
00:04:30.834 --> 00:04:32.747
And it never repacks a box.

77
00:04:32.747 --> 00:04:34.377
It only adds new files.

78
00:04:34.533 --> 00:04:43.137
That's why the card is an extension function in its own file: lamp.inventoryCard() reads like a member, and Lamp is untouched.

79
00:04:44.067 --> 00:04:53.272
Java has had this since Java 6: javac runs annotation processors, and KSP is Kotlin's own, faster way to do the same job.

80
00:04:53.272 --> 00:04:59.205
Java can even call the generated card, as a static method that takes the lamp.

81
00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:02.606
Should every card come from a helper?

82
00:05:02.606 --> 00:05:03.342
Not always.

83
00:05:03.342 --> 00:05:10.026
It pays off when the code repeats across many classes, runs often, or must be checked at build time.

84
00:05:10.026 --> 00:05:13.702
A test helper that prints any object a few dozen times?

85
00:05:13.702 --> 00:05:14.972
Reflection is fine.

86
00:05:15.133 --> 00:05:18.917
Never edit a generated file: the next build writes over it.

87
00:05:18.917 --> 00:05:22.701
And if your editor shows red before the first build, relax.

88
00:05:22.701 --> 00:05:24.753
The card just isn't written yet.

89
00:05:25.667 --> 00:05:28.622
So, who keeps the card in step with its class?

90
00:05:28.622 --> 00:05:29.521
Nobody has to.

91
00:05:29.521 --> 00:05:35.174
A helper reads every label on every build, and writes fresh cards before the van leaves.

92
00:05:35.174 --> 00:05:38.451
Only the writing moved, from a person to the build.

93
00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:39.399
Quick check.

94
00:05:39.399 --> 00:05:42.593
You add a property, colour, to Plant, and build.

95
00:05:42.593 --> 00:05:45.255
Which file changes, and in which folder?

96
00:05:48.467 --> 00:05:51.251
PlantInventoryCard.kt gains colour.

97
00:05:51.251 --> 00:05:56.501
It lives under build, generated, KSP, and nobody wrote it by hand.
